{"step_index":688,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T22:55:36Z","input_tokens":2563,"cache_read_tokens":171158,"output_tokens":2228,"thinking":"The structure of `rec` reveals key performance indicators: name, course section, raw score, switch violations, penalty points, final score, total questions, and timestamp. Examining these elements in combination might reveal patterns or correlations related to performance or testing behavior. The raw score, penalty, and final score fields point to a possible scoring calculation and I need to determine how the final score is computed.\n\n\nThe user's description clarifies the data flow: the system captures student name and course data, then formats the JSON output into human-readable text for display. This formatted output includes fields for name, course, score, violations, and time taken, which suggests a structured display method in the teacher's submissions page, potentially a table or list. The key calculation is still the final score based on the raw score and any applicable penalty.\n\n\nThe teacher's page auto-loads student submissions via a real-time Firestore listener, which requires read access to the submissions data. Initial attempts at reading the submissions failed due to insufficient permissions specified in the Firestore security rules. The root cause is `allow read: if false;` within the `submissions` rules, preventing data retrieval. The rule needs to be updated to `allow read, create: if true;` to permit the teacher's page to load data.\n\n\nThe updated Firestore rules now allow read access to the submissions, which should resolve the permission error. A friendly notification can be displayed on the teacher's page if read permissions are still denied. The student-submitted data fields include name, course, raw score, switch violations, penalty, final score, total questions, and timestamp, confirming the data structure needed for the teacher's display.\n\n\nThe initial data structure focused on internal processing, but now the focus shifts to creating a human-readable display. Formatted fields such as student name, course section, formatted score, violations, duration, and timestamp are crucial for the teacher's interface. Reviewing the Firestore rules, the updated rule set allows for the creation of new fields within `submissions`, without any key restrictions. The next step is validating this observation by testing the creation of a submission with additional fields.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"node -e \\\"\\nconst sub = {\\n  fields: {\\n    studentName: { stringValue: 'Test Student' },\\n    courseSection: { stringValue: 'BSIT 2-A' },\\n    scoreFormatted: { stringValue: '20 / 20' },\\n    submittedAt: { integerValue: String(Date.now()) }\\n  }\\n};\\nfetch('https://firestore.googleapis.com/v1/projects/sir-jasper-quiz/databases/(default)/documents/quizzes/active/submissions', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify(sub)\\n})\\n.then(r => r.json())\\n.then(d => console.log('Create submission response:', d.name ? 'SUCCESS: ' + d.name : d));\\n\\\"\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Test creating submission in Firestore\""}}]}
