{"step_index":690,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T22:55:58Z","input_tokens":2345,"cache_read_tokens":173730,"output_tokens":965,"thinking":"Submission creation was successful; all fields are permitted. The student's submission should encompass both the initial structured metrics and the formatted, human-readable fields. The formatted time includes minutes and seconds based on the elapsed duration.\n\n\nThe submission data is structured to include both raw numerical values and user-friendly text representations for each metric, ensuring data integrity and readability. The `teacher.html` page's \"Submissions\" tab loads data automatically from Firestore, specifically the submissions for the current quiz, ordered by submission timestamp. This is based on a real-time listener on the Firestore collection.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"firestore onsnapshot realtime submissions list table web javascript\""}}]}
{"step_index":691,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T22:56:08Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-09T06:56:08+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-09T06:56:12+08:00\nThe search for \"firestore onsnapshot realtime submissions list table web javascript\" returned the following summary:\nTo display a real-time updating list of **submissions** in an HTML table using **Firebase Firestore (`onSnapshot`)** and **JavaScript (Modular SDK v9/v10+)**, you can use the implementation below[1][2].\n\n### 1. HTML Structure\nCreate a basic HTML table with a `<tbody>` element that JavaScript can target and update dynamically[3].\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <title>Realtime Submissions Table</title>\n    <style>\n        table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: sans-serif; }\n        th, td { border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 8px; text-align: left; }\n        th { background-color: #f4f4f4; }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n    <h2>Recent Submissions</h2>\n    <table>\n        <thead>\n            <tr>\n                <th>Name</th>\n                <th>Email</th>\n                <th>Submitted At</th>\n            </tr>\n        </thead>\n        <tbody id=\"submissions-table-body\">\n            <!-- Real-time rows will inject here -->\n        </tbody>\n    </table>\n\n    <!-- Import your JavaScript file as a module -->\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"app.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n### 2. JavaScript Implementation (`app.js`)\nInitialize your Firestore database, hook up the `onSnapshot` listener to your `submissions` collection, and map the incoming documents directly into HTML table rows[1][4].\n\n```javascript\n// Import the necessary Firestore functions from the Firebase SDK\nimport { initializeApp } from \"firebase/app\";\nimport { getFirestore, collection, query, orderBy, onSnapshot } from \"firebase/firestore\";\n\n// 1. Your web app's Firebase configuration\nconst firebaseConfig = {\n    apiKey: \"YOUR_API_KEY\",\n    authDomain: \"YOUR_AUTH_DOMAIN\",\n    projectId: \"YOUR_PROJECT_ID\",\n    storageBucket: \"YOUR_STORAGE_BUCKET\",\n    messagingSenderId: \"YOUR_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID\",\n  \n<truncated 1500 bytes>\n{ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', \"'\": '&#39;', '\"': '&quot;' }[tag] || tag)\n    );\n}\n\n// Optional: Call unsubscribe() if you ever need to destroy/detach the listener \n// e.g., if the user navigates away from a Single Page Application (SPA) view.\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways:\n1. **`onSnapshot()`**: Fires immediately with the current state of the database and triggers again automatically whenever a document is added, updated, or deleted[1][4].\n2. **`tableBody.innerHTML = rowsHTML`**: Re-rendering the inner HTML template string on every snapshot update is clean for small-to-medium lists. For massive datasets, you may want to look into diff-checking individual snapshot changes (`snapshot.docChanges()`)[1].\n3. **Security (`escapeHTML`)**: Always sanitize user-generated inputs (like names or form text) before injecting them directly into `innerHTML` to prevent XSS attacks.\n\nSources:\n[1] [google.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEFCnR_g6kKs8t-rFTENF_cyYpPWATh-BL7CSlqsApZJeNY8Y4rMlAUPg8ELt9dmu8tvxRy3Psv2oVm0HLcgYpKkOm6fgDQMcE7Sx_279Q_jrBbdrUdp9UwnZt5NvWokiB-rXxsG7rNNWYOuf9qZwW1ECc=)\n[2] [freecodecamp.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH--0pPtgayj-6CD-8SS2NWW0En26ey2lY1cgqQPbe7pIButjk7EE-EqCIpViV7wR51PdyqHqrszf4JoEvo_iC3hYx5ciKcfJktr89hsXdx_gT7z0-v9Ab1XbrL0r1G0f3_pYs8LTYPwsyC7ccbD3ONrlby9kHNCdGU-X87NoCRWgNWt1VvRX15HA==)\n[3] [skcript.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFgwtKEDwQkTprbA9YABYk4VRpQNlF4J--C_ABPd55d_qcxWXJkmtKw_ooxcjvDB3RVUachjbdJOwbhL-iJwtuu9RVHV3cuoSMOSDhUJQtzN-jtXlRrzVt1qsX08O2Pb9c55W9I70YPCv15LxzD5J6aMO3pJPvhQCc=)\n[4] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.